QSPICE Validation Workflow: Mealy FSM Output vs Hardware Circuit Output
Outputs: TX_EN / TRIGGER / ALERT / ENC_VALID

1. Objective
This document provides a QSPICE-ready workflow to validate a Mealy finite-state machine (golden reference) against a real hardware (DUT) implementation for the output vector {TX_EN, TRIGGER, ALERT, ENC_VALID}. The validation produces an automatic PASS/FAIL result and a mismatch count.
2. High-level Method
Run the DUT and the golden Mealy model in parallel under identical digital inputs. At each clock cycle, compare each DUT output with its expected output. Any mismatch asserts a single flag MIS_ANY. PASS is declared if MIS_ANY stays low for the entire run.
3. Required Signals
Inputs (digital): VD_D, VP_D, ERR_D, CLK_D (and any additional inputs used in your paper).
Outputs (digital): TRIGGER_HW, TX_EN_HW, ENC_HW, ALERT_HW (from DUT) and TRIGGER_EXP, TX_EN_EXP, ENC_EXP, ALERT_EXP (from golden model).
4. Replace Xdut with an Existing Real-time Platform Subcircuit (Example)
Export your real platform as a QSPICE subcircuit (.SUBCKT). Then include it and instantiate it as Xdut. Below is an example for a deployed mixed-signal platform subcircuit named HOLOGRAM_PLATFORM_TOP. Replace the subcircuit name and pin order with your exported netlist.
; --- include the platform subcircuit exported from QSPICE schematic ---
.include "HOLOGRAM_PLATFORM_TOP.sub"

; --- instantiate DUT (real platform) ---
; Pin order example:
; Xdut VD_D VP_D CLK_D ERR_D  TRIGGER_HW TX_EN_HW ENC_HW ALERT_HW  HOLOGRAM_PLATFORM_TOP
Xdut VD_D VP_D CLK_D ERR_D  TRIGGER_HW TX_EN_HW ENC_HW ALERT_HW  HOLOGRAM_PLATFORM_TOP

5. Golden Mealy Reference Model (Edit δ and λ to match the paper)
Implement the Mealy machine in QSPICE using (i) a state register (DFFs) and (ii) behavioral equations for next-state (δ) and output (λ). A common Holo-circuit structure is: Idle → DepthReady → PoseReady → Fusion → OutputStable (final) and an Alert state on ERR. Below is a compact example template; update the state encodings and transitions to exactly match the paper.
; ---------------- Golden Mealy (template) ----------------
; Example state bits q2 q1 q0 (3-bit encoding)
; Define decoded state flags (edit encodings to match your paper)
BIDLE   IDLE 0   V={VDD*(1-u(V(q2)-1))*(1-u(V(q1)-1))*(1-u(V(q0)-1))}     ; 000
BDEPTH  DEP  0   V={VDD*(1-u(V(q2)-1))*(1-u(V(q1)-1))*u(V(q0)-1)}         ; 001
BPOSE   POS  0   V={VDD*(1-u(V(q2)-1))*u(V(q1)-1)*(1-u(V(q0)-1))}         ; 010
BFUSION FUS  0   V={VDD*(1-u(V(q2)-1))*u(V(q1)-1)*u(V(q0)-1)}             ; 011
BSTABLE STB  0   V={VDD*u(V(q2)-1)*(1-u(V(q1)-1))*(1-u(V(q0)-1))}         ; 100
BALRT   ALR  0   V={VDD*u(V(q2)-1)*(1-u(V(q1)-1))*u(V(q0)-1)}             ; 101

; Fused-valid (example): g(t)=VD_D & VP_D (edit if paper uses different gating)
Bg   G 0 V={VDD*u(V(VD_D)-1)*u(V(VP_D)-1)}

; Outputs λ(q,x) (edit to match your paper)
BTRIGGER_EXP TRIGGER_EXP 0 V={VDD*u(V(FUS)-1)*u(V(G)-1)}                  ; TRIGGER ≡ GAND_EN in Fusion
BALERT_EXP   ALERT_EXP   0 V={VDD*u(V(ERR_D)-1)}                          ; ALERT on ERR
BTX_EN_EXP   TX_EN_EXP   0 V={VDD*u(V(STB)-1)*u(V(CLK_D)-1)}              ; TX_EN pulse in OutputStable
BENC_EXP     ENC_EXP     0 V={VDD*u(V(TX_EN_EXP)-1)}                      ; ENC_VALID = TX_EN (example)

; Next-state δ(q,x): implement D2,D1,D0 equations (edit rules to match the paper)
; Then clock them into q2,q1,q0 using DFFs (preferred) or edge-sampled storage.

6. Output Comparators and MIS_ANY Flag
Compare DUT vs expected outputs using absolute difference (or XOR after thresholding). Then OR all mismatches into MIS_ANY. The run PASSes if MIS_ANY stays low.
; ------------- Mismatch detection -------------
BMIS_TRIG MIS_TRIG 0 V=if(abs(V(TRIGGER_HW)-V(TRIGGER_EXP))>VTH,VDD,0)
BMIS_TX   MIS_TX   0 V=if(abs(V(TX_EN_HW)-V(TX_EN_EXP))>VTH,VDD,0)
BMIS_ENC  MIS_ENC  0 V=if(abs(V(ENC_HW)-V(ENC_EXP))>VTH,VDD,0)
BMIS_ALR  MIS_ALR  0 V=if(abs(V(ALERT_HW)-V(ALERT_EXP))>VTH,VDD,0)

BMIS_ANY MIS_ANY 0 V=if( V(MIS_TRIG)>VTH | V(MIS_TX)>VTH | V(MIS_ENC)>VTH | V(MIS_ALR)>VTH, VDD, 0 )

7. Automatic PASS/FAIL and Mismatch Count in QSPICE
Use .meas to compute MIS_MAX and estimate the number of mismatched cycles. PASS is 1 if MIS_MAX < VTH.
; ------------- Measurements -------------
.meas tran MIS_MAX  max   V(MIS_ANY) from=0 to=TSTOP
.meas tran MIS_AREA integ V(MIS_ANY) from=0 to=TSTOP
.meas tran MIS_CNT  param='MIS_AREA/(VDD*TCLK)'
.meas tran PASS     param='(MIS_MAX<VTH)'

8. What to Deliver as Validation Output (for the paper)
Include the following plots/screenshots from QSPICE:
•	Overlaid outputs: TRIGGER_HW vs TRIGGER_EXP, TX_EN_HW vs TX_EN_EXP, ENC_HW vs ENC_EXP, ALERT_HW vs ALERT_EXP.
•	Mismatch flag MIS_ANY (should remain 0 for normal/real case).
•	Fraud/attack case: show MIS_ANY = 1 during the fault window (e.g., ERR asserted or injected glitch).
•	QSPICE measurement results: MIS_MAX, MIS_CNT, PASS.
9. For Real Hardware Platforms
If the DUT outputs are registered (one-cycle latency), delay the expected outputs by one clock or compare only at sampling edges. Additionally, it should be ensured the same digitization thresholds are used for analog-to-digital conversions.
